# `owned sqlite3_free` hands the freeing to the compiler, so the program calling
# it too frees the string twice — exit 0 and silent on both platforms, which is
# the worst answer a memory bug can give (§4.19, panel 109's condition 2, the
# one four seats wrote independently).
#
# Written for the class and not for one witness: the mark rides a RESULT here,
# and the refusal reads the callee's name, so it fires the same way for a mark
# on a `@` parameter. Its two negative shapes are in `selfhost/check/freer.hero`
# — no mark at all, and a mark naming a different freer — because a refusal
# that cannot be turned off is not a refusal, it is a ban.
extern "sqlite3.h" link "sqlite3"
    function sqlite3_errmsg(db: ptr) -> cstr owned sqlite3_free
    function sqlite3_free(p: ptr)

function main()
    sqlite3_free(nullptr)  #~ owned_freer_called
    print(0)
